 {"time_utc": "08:30", "currency": "USD", "title": "Durable Goods Orders m/m", "impact": "Low"}], "editorial": "EUR/USD trades at 1.14039 as the European session gives way to the New York open, sitting in a position that the desk reads as increasingly precarious for the single currency. The daily chart has spent the better part of three weeks carving out what looks like a distribution pattern beneath a cluster of resistance zones, and the price action across the past five sessions has done little to challenge that interpretation.\n\nThe structure story is one of fading momentum. EUR/USD printed a session high of 1.14818 on 15 July \u2014 the most extended push in the recent range \u2014 before sellers reasserted themselves. Since that peak, the daily candles have traced a sequence of lower highs and, more recently, lower lows. The 23 July candle was particularly telling: a range of roughly 72 pips that closed near its lows at 1.13779, followed by two further sessions of compressed, lower closes. That kind of behaviour beneath a prior reaction zone at 1.14461 \u2014 which has now registered three touches \u2014 is consistent with supply absorbing demand rather than the reverse. The 1.14365 zone, also carrying three touches, sits just above current price and represents the more immediate overhead reference. A sustained failure to reclaim that level on a daily closing basis would reinforce the bearish read materially.\n\nOn the H1 chart, the intraday narrative has been one of a quiet overnight base followed by a European session recovery. Price ground from lows near 1.13636 in the early hours, rallying through the morning to print 1.14143 around the 11:00\u201312:00 UTC window. Since then, the move has stalled and price has drifted back to the 1.14039 area, suggesting the recovery has met supply in the vicinity of the 1.14143 intraday high. The H1 structure shows a series of higher lows from the Asian session low, but the inability to sustain gains above 1.14100 into the early afternoon is notable. The prior reaction zone at 1.14365 remains well above current price and would need to be meaningfully breached on an H1 closing basis before the intraday picture shifts.\n\nToday's calendar is light on high-impact risk. The German ifo Business Climate, M3 Money Supply, and Private Loans data all crossed at 04:00 UTC with low-impact designations, and the ECOFIN meeting is unlikely to generate sharp directional flow. US Durable Goods Orders at 08:30 UTC carry a low-impact flag, though a meaningful miss or beat on the core reading could introduce a brief volatility spike. In the current environment, that is more likely to generate noise than a structural shift.\n\nWhat would change the desk's bearish read? A daily close above the 1.14461 prior reaction zone would challenge the distribution thesis and suggest buyers are regaining structural control. A push through 1.14827 on a closing basis would invalidate the broader bearish framing entirely. Until either of those scenarios materialises, the weight of evidence sits with the sellers.\n\nThis is editorial analysis, not financial advice. Trading involves substantial risk of loss.", "sample": false}